  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Dialogen over Belgi\u00eb shows early scholarly uptake alongside strong signs of use. It has been cited once, downloaded 1,282 times through OAPEN, and is available open access in multiple repository instances, suggesting broad reach for a book focused on Belgian collective memory, identity, and public discourse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2020 Leuven University Press book brings together scholars from humanities, philosophy, and political science to explore how prominent Belgians remember their country through places, figures, and shared events. The record points to a work positioned at the intersection of memory studies and questions of social and political belonging, with explicit alignment to \u201cReduced inequalities\u201d in the SDG profile.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest uptake signal is in usage: the book has been downloaded 1,282 times in OAPEN, indicating sustained interest rather than a one-time spike. Its open access availability through the publisher, OAPEN, and KU Leuven\u2019s repository likely supports that reach and makes the book easier to discover and use across scholarly and educational settings.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence is still modest but meaningful: one citing work appears in 2025 in Memory Studies, authored by Val\u00e9rie Rosoux and centered on family memory, silences, and narrative analysis. That citation suggests the book is already feeding into specialized research on memory and identity, especially within Belgian academic discourse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding record is consistent with a book that is being read and entering scholarly conversation, while remaining early in its citation life. Library holdings and subject classifications in society and culture, local history, and interdisciplinary studies further suggest that it is being framed as a resource for research and teaching, even as the citation base is still small.</p>",
